    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d858">

                <pb facs="0992" n="880" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg880"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.113 (Manchuria) Petroleum/78: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-09T11:00:00+09:00">February 9, 1935—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 9—1:58 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>29. Department’s 376, December 6, 2 p.m., to Peiping,<anchor n="75"
                        xml:id="d858-f1"/> discrimination in regard to Manchurian import tariff on
                    kerosene.</p>
                <pb facs="0993" n="881" xml:id="pg881"/>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Because of unsatisfactory nature of the reply of the Manchurian
                        authorities to the representations made on December 19 by the British Consul
                        General at Mukden, the British Foreign Office has instructed the British
                        Ambassador in Tokyo,<anchor n="76" xml:id="d858-f2"/> after consultation
                        with me, to make representations to the Japanese authorities in an endeavor
                        to enlist their assistance in remedying the situation.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The British Ambassador proposes to make representations somewhat along the
                        following lines: <quote rend="blockquote">
                            <p>“Although the Manchurian authorities have promised an improvement in
                                the situation the foreign oil companies are still anxious as to the
                                future and therefore the British Government hope that the Japanese
                                Government will see their way clear to impress upon the Manchurian
                                authorities the necessity for more thoroughgoing measures.”</p>
                        </quote></item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>The British Ambassador or Sansom will make these representations alone if
                        necessary but prefer that we take similar and concurrent action. I approve
                        the action indicated.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Please instruct.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
